// run
// #2101: `catch e` bound the caught error unconditionally at the predeclared
// `error` interface (SPEC §10.6), even when the callee's signature named a
// concrete error type E via `T!E` (SPEC §18.2). `fail` and `?` both honour
// the declared E; only the catch binder erased it, so `e`'s own fields or
// variants were unreachable through `e` and had to be recovered with a
// runtime `e.(E)` type assertion. Two nominal shapes E can take, in one
// program: a class (field access) and an enum (`match` dispatch on `e`'s
// variants). Before the fix, `e.code` was E0057 ("no field or method 'code'
// on this type") and `match (e)` was E0069 ("'match' requires an enum
// subject, found 'error'").
// A single-file golden gets NO prelude, so the class error type's
// `message()` method (§10.6) is declared locally rather than pulled from
// `std/errors`. The enum error type (`Kind`) has none: a method receiver
// must be a class or type-alias (SPEC §10.4), never an enum, and this
// program never calls `.message()` on `e` in the enum case — it only
// `match`es it — so none is needed.
//
// `g` fails with the enum's SECOND variant deliberately, not the first:
// filing #2939 while writing this case found that `fail`ing a bare enum's
// discriminant-0 variant is swallowed as "no error" by the unrelated
// runtime nil-sentinel check in `catch`/`?`. That bug is orthogonal to this
// one (it reproduces with `catch <default>`, which never touches a binder)
// and stays out of scope here.
class MyErr {
  code: int
  message(): string { return "code ${this.code}" }
}
fn f(): int!MyErr { fail MyErr{ code: 7 } }

enum Kind { NotFound, Invalid }
fn g(): int!Kind { fail Kind.Invalid }

fn main() {
  let v1 = f() catch e {
    println("class: code is ${e.code}")
    0
  }
  println("v1=${v1}")

  let v2 = g() catch e {
    match (e) {
      NotFound => println("enum: not found")
      Invalid => println("enum: invalid")
    }
    0
  }
  println("v2=${v2}")
}
